The files in `rules/` are unmodified. The filter engine in `src/` is an
independent implementation and is not derived from RTK source code.

Only RTK's declarative filter corpus is vendored. RTK also implements per-command
filtering in Rust under `src/cmds/`, where a module both executes the command and
shapes its output, and in one case substitutes a different command line than the
caller wrote. None of that is vendored or ported. This crate is a pure output
transformation: it never spawns a process and never rewrites a command, because
execution and authorization belong to the shell tool group.

One vendored rule refers to that absent layer. The description in
`rules/spring-boot.toml` states that `mvn spring-boot:run` is routed to a
dedicated Maven filter and never reaches the Spring Boot rule. Upstream that
filter is a Rust module, so the statement does not hold here and the command does
reach the Spring Boot rule. That is the better fit for application logs, so
`rules-workcell/maven.toml` matches Maven goals explicitly rather than the `mvn`
program in order to preserve the routing.

The rules in `rules-workcell/` are original to this project and are not derived
from RTK. They are kept in a separate directory so that refreshing the vendored
corpus stays a clean copy of `rules/`, and so that provenance is visible without
reading individual files. Both directories are concatenated at build time and a
file name may not appear in both.

Behavioral changes relative to RTK, applied by this engine rather than by
editing the vendored rules:

  * `on_empty` and `match_output` short-circuit messages are suppressed when the
    command exited non-zero, so a failing command is never reported as success.
  * Rules are matched against a normalized command scope produced by
    tree-sitter Bash parsing rather than the raw command string.
  * Only the built-in rule set is loaded. Project-local and user-global rule
    files are deliberately unsupported.
  * Two reductions are command-independent and have no counterpart upstream:
    terminal redraw rendering, which decodes a carriage-return control stream
    into the rows a terminal would show, and progress line collapse, which
    reduces runs of frames that arrive one per line. Neither is selected by
    `match_command`, because the programs that emit progress bars are
    overwhelmingly ones the corpus does not name.
